    "conflict": false,
    "discussionSummary": "Consensus A: probes stop unready Pods receiving traffic, which halts a bad rollout. Commenters also note GKE Deployments are not managed instance groups.",
    "conceptSummary": "Using readiness probes to make rolling updates fail safe.",
    "correctRationale": "A rolling update replaces Pods gradually and only proceeds as new Pods become Ready. A readiness probe that exercises the application's actual dependencies will fail when a production-only parameter is misconfigured, so the new Pod is never added to the Service endpoints and the rollout stalls with the old Pods still serving. The result is a stuck deployment instead of an outage, which is exactly the preventive control the platform needs.",
    "optionRationales": [
      "Correct: Readiness probes withhold traffic from Pods that are not genuinely healthy and stall the rolling update before old Pods are removed, turning a bad release into a halted rollout.",
      "Wrong: GKE Deployments manage Pods through ReplicaSets, not managed instance groups, so this control does not apply to the workload in question.",
      "Wrong: A scheduled availability check detects the outage after it has already happened; it prevents nothing.",
      "Wrong: An uptime alert notifies you once the application is already down, which is detection rather than prevention."
    ]
