    "discussionSummary": "Consensus C: hotspots in Bigtable are caused by poor row key design, and spreading keys evenly is the documented remedy.",
    "conceptSummary": "Diagnosing and preventing Bigtable hotspots through row key design.",
    "correctRationale": "Bigtable splits data into tablets by contiguous row key range and assigns tablets to nodes. When keys are sequential or share a common leading value, such as a timestamp or a single device prefix, all current traffic lands in one range and therefore on one node, producing a hotspot regardless of cluster size. Redesigning the row key so values distribute evenly across the key space spreads load across all nodes, which both resolves the current problem and prevents recurrence.",
    "optionRationales": [
      "Wrong: The client library or API surface used has no effect on how rows are distributed across tablets and nodes.",
      "Wrong: Deleting old records reduces stored volume but does nothing about live traffic concentrating on a single key range.",
      "Correct: Hotspots are a row key design problem; distributing keys evenly across the key space spreads load over all nodes and prevents recurrence.",
      "Wrong: Adding nodes cannot help when traffic targets one key range, because that range still lives on a single node. It raises cost without fixing the cause."
    ]
