    "discussionSummary": "Split between C and D. Supporters of C note Google load balancers natively support websockets and that the task says help it run, not redesign. Supporters of D argue non-distributed sessions are a design flaw that no load balancer fixes, and the app is still in design.",
    "conceptSummary": "Externalizing session state so an application can scale horizontally.",
    "correctRationale": "State held in the memory of one web server means any request routed elsewhere loses the session, and any instance replacement, autoscale-in or zone failure destroys live user sessions. Load balancers can paper over this with session affinity, but affinity degrades load distribution and still loses state when the instance goes away. Because the application is still in design, moving session state to a distributed service such as Memorystore makes the tier stateless and lets it scale and self-heal properly.",
