    "discussionSummary": "Consensus D: hash the local files and compare with the CRC32C already exposed by ls -L. Downloading the data back would incur egress cost and time.",
    "conceptSummary": "Verifying upload integrity without re-downloading the data.",
    "correctRationale": "Cloud Storage computes and stores a CRC32C checksum for every object at upload time, and gsutil ls -L exposes it as metadata. Computing the same checksum locally and comparing the two values proves the bytes match, using only metadata requests. Nothing is transferred back, so there are no egress charges and no waiting for a second full transfer, which is exactly what minimizing cost and effort means here.",
