    "discussionSummary": "Consensus B, with an important caveat: Pub/Sub has since added ordering keys and exactly-once delivery, though scoped per region. Because the frontend is global, commenters argue Dataflow is still needed for cross-region ordering and deduplication.",
    "conceptSummary": "Achieving ordered, exactly-once delivery for an order-sensitive consumer.",
    "correctRationale": "The legacy API requires strict chronological order and no duplicates, which a globally distributed publisher cannot guarantee on its own. A Dataflow streaming pipeline between Pub/Sub and the API buffers events into windows, uses watermarks to wait for late arrivals, sorts by event time and deduplicates by message identifier before emitting. Note that Pub/Sub has since gained ordering keys and exactly-once delivery, but those guarantees are scoped within a region, which is why a global frontend still benefits from the Dataflow stage.",
