    "confidence": "high",
    "conflict": false,
    "discussionSummary": "Consensus B: table expiration would destroy the whole table, while partition expiration prunes only the old data.",
    "conceptSummary": "Partition expiration for rolling data retention in BigQuery.",
    "correctRationale": "A time-partitioned table splits rows into daily partitions by ingestion or event time. Setting a partition expiration of 45 days makes BigQuery delete each partition automatically once it passes that age, so old logs disappear while the table and its recent partitions remain. Partitioning also reduces query cost, since queries filtered by date scan only the relevant partitions.",
    "optionRationales": [
      "Wrong: Table expiration deletes the entire table once it reaches the age, taking the recent logs with it and leaving the application with nowhere to write.",
      "Correct: Partition expiration prunes only partitions older than 45 days, leaving the table and current data intact, and partitioning also lowers query cost.",
      "Wrong: BigQuery has no default behaviour that automatically deletes data after any period; retention must be configured.",
      "Wrong: A custom deletion script is operational overhead and DML cost for something the service does natively as a table property."
    ]
