    "source": "gemini-validated",
    "sourceExam": "professional-cloud-architect",
    "sourceQuestionNumber": 70,
    "isRecent": false,
    "importBatch": "examtopics-2026-08",
    "confidence": "high",
    "conflict": false,
    "discussionSummary": "Consensus D, with the note that modern Cloud SQL simply calls this enabling high availability rather than creating a failover replica. Read replicas explicitly provide no failover capability.",
    "conceptSummary": "Cloud SQL high availability is same-region, cross-zone.",
    "correctRationale": "Cloud SQL high availability provisions a standby in a second zone of the same region and replicates synchronously, so no committed transaction is lost and failover is automatic when the primary zone fails. Read replicas replicate asynchronously and exist to serve read traffic; the documentation states explicitly that a primary cannot fail over to one. Cross-region configurations are disaster recovery rather than high availability, because the replication is asynchronous.",
    "optionRationales": [
      "Wrong: Read replicas serve read traffic and cannot be failed over to, so they provide no high availability regardless of location.",
      "Wrong: Cloud SQL high availability operates within a region; a cross-region arrangement is disaster recovery with asynchronous replication.",
      "Wrong: Placing a read replica in another zone still gives no failover capability, only additional read capacity.",
      "Correct: A standby in another zone of the same region with synchronous replication and automatic failover is exactly Cloud SQL high availability."
    ],
    "legacyNote": "Cloud SQL ya no usa el término «failover replica»: hoy simplemente se activa la alta disponibilidad, que aprovisiona el standby en otra zona de la misma región."
