    "source": "gemini-validated",
    "sourceExam": "professional-cloud-architect",
    "sourceQuestionNumber": 67,
    "isRecent": false,
    "importBatch": "examtopics-2026-08",
    "confidence": "high",
    "conflict": false,
    "discussionSummary": "Consensus C: it is the only option that packs many small containers per node for utilization and tests in staging before promoting to production.",
    "conceptSummary": "Bin-packing small workloads and promoting version-tagged images.",
    "correctRationale": "An application needing 0.1 CPU and 128 MB would waste almost an entire VM if given one, so maximizing utilization means running many replicas per machine, which is exactly what Kubernetes scheduling does using the declared requests. Reliability comes from tagging each image with a version, which makes the running artifact unambiguous, and from deploying to a staging namespace first and promoting the same tested image to production afterwards.",
    "optionRationales": [
      "Wrong: Installing many dependencies in a startup script makes every boot slow and fragile, and restarting instances to deploy gives no staging validation or rollback.",
      "Wrong: A full n1-standard-1 for a workload needing 0.1 CPU wastes most of the machine, which directly contradicts maximizing utilization.",
      "Correct: GKE packs many small containers per node for high utilization, and version-tagged images promoted from staging to production give reliable, reversible releases.",
      "Wrong: n1-standard-4 nodes over-provision further, and the latest tag with an always pull policy makes it impossible to know which version is running or to roll back."
    ]
